<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saalfeld</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saalfeld/Saale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saalfeld is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. An old mining and trading town, it was the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Saalfeld and then of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld until 1826, when it passed to Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>. On 10 October 1806 the Battle of Saalfeld, in which Prince Louis Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was killed, opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s campaign against Prussia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saalfeld.jpg" alt="Saalfeld" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Sander · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stadtkirche_Saalfeld.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
